body: "## Measured state (2026-07-25)\n\nRow-streaming coverage across the row-producing read surfaces:\n\n| surface | `--stream` | `--format ndjson` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `list` | yes | yes |\n| `search` | no | yes |\n| `context` | no | yes (json/ndjson only) |\n| `next` | no | no |\n| `activity` | no | no |\n| `graph` | no | no |\n| `aggregate` | no | no |\n| `deps` | no | no |\n| `get` | no | no (json/toon) |\n\n`events` is natively newline-delimited and is the shape everything else should converge on.\n\nSo there are three different answers to \"give me rows I can pipe\" — a dedicated `--stream` boolean, a `--format ndjson` enum value, and nothing at all — and the surfaces with no answer are precisely the ones that produce the largest row sets at scale: `graph` (4,352 edges today, millions later), `activity`, `deps`, `aggregate`.\n\n## Why this is a scale primitive, not a flag tidy-up\n\nAt a million items the array-envelope shape is not merely verbose, it is unusable: both producer and consumer must materialize the full result before the first row is readable. `jq` on a one-million-element array buffers the array; `head -20` cannot terminate the producer early; an agent cannot start work on row 1 until row 1,000,000 is serialized. Line-delimited rows make the pipeline incremental in constant memory on both sides, and make `head`/`grep -m`/`xargs -n` terminate the producer through SIGPIPE.\n\nThis is the mechanical foundation of \"pm composes with grep/jq/xargs\" (pm-m9xv) at the scale pm-9rxu targets.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. One contract-declared streaming mode across every row-producing read; `--stream` and `--format ndjson` both remain valid spellings (alias-preserved, zero feature loss) but resolve to one implementation and one documented row schema per surface.\n2. Rows are emitted incrementally as they are produced — a real generator/async-iterator path through the SDK, not a materialized array printed line by line. Gate this behaviourally: a bounded-memory test streams a large synthetic workspace with a heap ceiling, and an early-termination test asserts the producer stops on a closed pipe.\n3. Envelope metadata (counts, cursors, truncation) that has no place in a row stream is emitted as a final trailer record with a declared discriminator key, so a stream is still self-describing and resumable via `--after`.\n4. SIGPIPE/EPIPE handling is silent and exit-code-correct (the pm-psy1 shell contract governs the codes).\n\n## Relationship to neighbours\n\n- pm-srns owns the format-flag grammar and envelope spec — this item is the streaming shape that grammar must express, not a competing contract.\n- pm-psy1 owns exit codes and stream discipline; the EPIPE behaviour is specified there.\n- pm-plwm `--where` predicates keep selection O(result); streaming keeps delivery O(1) memory. Both are needed for million-item pipelines.\n- pm-tuadp2 `--since` delta reads and pm-5gf0xf `--at` historical projection both produce row sets that inherit this shape."
